        "high altitude",
        "female sex"
      ]
    },
    "pathophysiology": "The ductus arteriosus, which links the pulmonary artery to the aorta in the fetus, fails to close after birth. Blood shunts from the aorta into the pulmonary artery, overloading the lungs and left heart. Prostaglandin E2 keeps the duct open; rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins normally close it.",
    "timeline": {
      "onset": "Days after birth, as pulmonary resistance falls",
      "course": "Small ducts may close in the first months; large ones cause heart failure or, untreated, Eisenmenger syndrome"
    },
    "key_findings": {
      "history": [
        "poor feeding",
        "fast breathing",
        "failure to thrive"
      ],
      "exam": [
        "continuous machine-like murmur below the left clavicle",
        "bounding pulses with a wide pulse pressure",
        "differential cyanosis in Eisenmenger syndrome: blue toes, pink fingers"
      ],
      "labs": [],
      "imaging": [
        "echocardiogram showing the duct and the shunt",
        "chest X-ray with increased pulmonary vascular markings"
      ]
    },
    "diagnosis": {
      "confirmatory_tests": [
        "echocardiogram"
      ],
      "criteria": []
    },
    "management": {
      "first_line": [
        "indomethacin, ibuprofen or acetaminophen to close a hemodynamically significant duct in premature infants"
      ],
      "next_line": [
        "catheter or surgical closure for a large duct that persists or when drugs fail"
      ]
    },
    "complications": [
      "heart failure",
      "infective endarteritis",
      "Eisenmenger syndrome with differential cyanosis"
    ],
    "confused_with": [
      {
        "script_id": "ventricular-septal-defect",
        "how_to_tell_apart": "A PDA gives a continuous machine-like murmur below the left clavicle with bounding pulses; a VSD gives a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border."
      }
    ],
    "pearls": [
      "Indomethacin closes the duct; prostaglandin E1 keeps it open.",
      "Congenital rubella classically causes PDA, cataracts and deafness.",
      "Eisenmenger syndrome from a PDA turns the toes blue but not the fingers, because deoxygenated blood enters the aorta after the arch vessels."
    ],
